🌱 Spring & Spring Boot – Structured Notes

1. What is Spring?

Spring is a Java framework used to build enterprise-level applications.

It provides loose coupling through Dependency Injection (DI) and supports modular development.

The Inversion of Control (IoC) container manages object creation and their lifecycles.


🔑 Core Concepts of Spring

1. Dependency Injection (DI)

Definition: Injecting objects into a class instead of creating them manually.

Managed by the IoC container.

Classes annotated with @Component, @Service, @Repository, etc. are registered as beans.



2. Inversion of Control (IoC)

Instead of the developer controlling object creation, the framework controls it.

This is called externalization of control → shifting control from developer → framework.



3. Bean

An object managed by the IoC container.

Defined either via annotations (@Component, @Bean) or XML (legacy).



4. ApplicationContext

The implementation of the IoC container.

Responsible for loading bean definitions, wiring them, and managing their lifecycle.





---

2. What is Spring Boot?

Spring Boot is a framework built on top of Spring.

It simplifies application setup by removing boilerplate configuration.


Why Spring Boot?

Auto-configuration → automatically configures beans based on dependencies.

Embedded servers → ships with Tomcat, Jetty, Undertow (no need to deploy WAR separately).

Starter dependencies → bundles required libraries (e.g., spring-boot-starter-web).

Production-ready features → health checks, monitoring with Actuator.

Convention over configuration → sensible defaults, minimal XML.



---

3. Architecture of Spring & Spring Boot

Spring Architecture (Layered)

1. Core Container

Beans, Core, Context, Expression Language.



2. Data Access Layer

JDBC, ORM, Transactions.



3. Web Layer

Web, WebMVC, WebSocket.



4. AOP Layer

Aspect-Oriented Programming.



5. Integration Layer

Messaging, Scheduling, Email.




Spring Boot Architecture

Spring Boot Starter → brings all dependencies.

Auto Configuration → sets up beans automatically.

Spring Boot CLI → run apps from command line.

Embedded Server (Tomcat/Jetty/Undertow).

Spring Actuator → monitoring endpoints.



